<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-ratio-root" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Ratio and Root Tests</title>

  <p>In <xref ref="sec-comparison"/> we saw how comparing to another series that
  we know converges or diverges is a helpful technique.  In this section,
  we introduce two tools for comparing to a geometric series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty r^n</m>
  without having to explicitly state what <m>r</m> is in advance.
  </p>

  <example>
    <p>
      Consider the series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \frac{n}{2^n}</m>.
      Note that we cannot use a direct or limit comparison to the geometric series
      <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \frac{1}{2^n}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      However, one other way to characterize the geometric series is that the
      ratio between successive terms is <m>r</m>.  For this series, we have
      <md>\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}=\dfrac{\frac{(n+1)}{2^{n+1}}}{\frac{n}{2^n}}=\left(\dfrac{n+1}{n}\right)\dfrac{1}{2}</md>.
      Thus, we see that the ratio in these terms is approaching <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>, just like
      a geometric series.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key idea is that since the ratio is approaching <m>\frac{1}{2} \lt 1</m>, we can eventually
      ensure the ratio stays strictly less than <m>1</m> and argue for convergence.
    </p>
  </example>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-ratio-test">
    <title>Ratio Test</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>\sum _{n=1} ^\infty a_n</m> be a series with <m>a_n \neq 0</m> for all <m>n</m>.
        If the limit exists, set
        <md>L=\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|</md>.
        Then
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>L \lt 1</m>, then the series converges absolutely.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If <m>L \gt 1</m> (or the limit is infinite), then the series diverges.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol> 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        First Suppose <m>L \gt 1</m>. Then for some <m>N</m>,
        we have 
        <m>\frac{|a_{n+1}|}{|a_n|} \gt 1</m> for all <m>n \geq N</m>.
        , so we have <m>|a_{n}| \gt |a_N| \gt 0</m> for all <m>n \gt N</m>.
        Hence the terms <m>a_n</m> do not approach zero, so the series diverges
        by <xref ref="prop-terms-zero"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now suppose <m>L\lt 1</m>. Set <m>r=\frac{1+L}{2}</m>, so that 
        <m>L\lt r\lt 1</m>. Since <m>r-L \gt 0</m>, by the definition of the limit, there is
        a positive integer <m>M</m> such that, whenever <m>n\geq M</m>,
      <md>
          \left|\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|-L\right|\lt r-L,
      </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
      Note that if <m>\left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| \lt L</m>, we have
      <m>\left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| \lt L \lt r</m>,
      and if <m>\left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right| \gt L</m>,
      then 
      <md>
        \left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|-L=\left|\left|\dfrac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|-L\right| \lt r-L
      </md>.
      So in either case we obtain 
          <m>\left|\frac{a_{n+1}}{a_n}\right|\lt r</m> for all <m>n \geq M</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <m>n&gt;M</m>, multiplying these bounds gives
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          |a_n|
          =|a_M|\frac{|a_{M+1}|}{|a_M|}
          \cdots\frac{|a_n|}{|a_{n-1}|}
          \lt |a_M|r^{n-M}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Let <m>S_k</m> be the <m>k</m>-th partial sum <m>S_k=\sum_{n=1}^{k}|a_n|</m>.
        For <m>k \gt M</m>,we have 
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          S_k
          \amp = \sum _{n=1} ^M |a_n|+\sum_{n=M+1}^k |a_n|
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \lt S_M+\sum_{n={M+1}}^{k}|a_M|r^{n-M}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = S_M+|a_M| \sum_{j=1}^{k-M}r^{j}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp =S_M+|a_M|\frac{r(1-r^{k-M})}{1-r}
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp \leq S_M+\frac{|a_M|r}{1-r}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Thus the absolute partial sums are increasing and bounded,
        so they converge. Therefore, the original series converges absolutely.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <activity>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Use the ratio test to determine if the following series converge or diverge.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{n^2}{3^n}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{n^3-n+1}{n!}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=2} ^\infty \dfrac{(-3)^n}{n^2-1}</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </activity>

  <p>The ratio test relies on successive terms to behave
  geometrically.  Some series can exhibit geometric series-like
  decay more broadly while successive terms are misbehaving.
  </p>
  <example>
    <p>
      Consider the series <m>\displaystyle \sum _{n=1} ^\infty \dfrac{1}{3^{n+(-1)^n}}</m>.
      Note that the terms of the series look like
      <md>S_8 = 1+\dfrac{1}{3^3}+\dfrac{1}{3^2}+\dfrac{1}{3^5}+\dfrac{1}{3^4}+\dfrac{1}{3^7}+\dfrac{1}{3^6}+\dfrac{1}{3^9}
      </md>,
      so the series definitely feels geometric
    </p>
    <p>
      However, if we try to compute ratios of successive terms, we have
      <md>\dfrac{a_{2n}}{a_{2n-1}}=\dfrac{\frac{1}{3^{2n+1}}}{\frac{1}{3^{2n-2}}}=\frac{1}{3^3}</md>
      while
      <md>\dfrac{a_{2n+1}}{a_{2n}}=\dfrac{\frac{1}{3^{2n}}}{\frac{1}{3^{2n+1}}}=\frac{1}{3}</md>.
      Thus the limit of ratios of successive terms does not exist.
    </p>
  </example>
  

</section>